.TH IOSTAT 1M
.SH NAME
iostat \- report I/O statistics
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B iostat
[
.B \-t
] [
.B \-i
]
[ interval [ count ] ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Iostat
delves into the system and reports certain statistics kept about
input-output activity.
For typewriters collectively, the number of input and output characters
are counted.
Also, on each clock tick,
a tally is made in one of 32 slots, chosen by which devices had an
exchange outstanding crossed with whether the system was
executing in user mode, in `nice' (background)
user mode, in system mode, or idle.
Each of the four categories reported below is therefore the sum of
eight of those slots, and each device's busy share is the sum of the
slots with its own bit set.
.PP
The optional
.I interval
argument causes
.I iostat
to report once each
.I interval
seconds.
The first report is for all time since a reboot and each
subsequent report is for the last interval only.
.PP
The optional
.I count
argument restricts the number of reports.
With an
.I interval
and no
.IR count ,
.I iostat
reports until it is stopped.
With neither, it prints one report and exits.
.PP
With no option argument
.I iostat
gives, for each instrumented device, the percentage of time it had an
exchange outstanding
.RB ( %bsy ),
the exchanges it completed per second
.RB ( tps )
and the words those exchanges moved per second
.RB ( wps );
and then the percentage of time the system has
spent in each of the four categories mentioned above.
.PP
There are two devices.
.B MD
is the magnetic disk of
.BR kernel/dev/md.c ,
.B all of its units together,
and
.B MB
is the drum, which is also the paging store.
.PP
The following options are available:
.TP
.B \-t
Report the number of characters of terminal IO per second as well.
.TP
.B \-i
Report the percentage of time spent in each
of the four categories mentioned above, one per line and named.
.SH FILES
None.
.I Iostat
reads
.BR dk_time ,
.BR dk_numb ,
.BR dk_wds ,
.B tk_nin
and
.B tk_nout
through
.IR kctl (2)
and opens no device.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
kctl(2), vmstat(1), pstat(1M), ps(1)
.SH BUGS
.PP
.B Note:
.B a device is a controller, not a drive.
v7's
.I iostat
named three disks and reported milliseconds per seek and per transfer for
each.
.B dk_busy
is three bits wide here because it is the low three bits of the subscript
.B dk_time
is tallied by, and
.B kernel/dev/md.c
addresses 64 drives; so
.B MD
is the disks together and
.B MB
the drums together, and a per-drive breakdown would need a wider histogram
than
.B dk_time
is.
Seek and transfer times are not reported at all: the controller returns no
timing, and
.B wps
says what those columns were for.
There is no
.B cp_time
on this system and none is wanted \-
.B dk_time
is it.
.PP
.B Note:
v7's
.B \-s
(the 32 raw slots) and
.B \-b
(buffer-cache statistics, out of an
.B io_info
structure this kernel does not have) are gone, as are the
undocumented
.B \-d
and
.BR \-a .
.PP
.B Note:
the clock runs at
.B HZ
ticks per second, which is 250 here and was 60 on the PDP-11.
Nothing in the report is expressed in ticks, so this shows up only in
the resolution of the percentages.
.PP
Things can change while
.I iostat
is running.
